WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.236
Welcome to Grade 3 English, Unit 3: Time and Daily Life.

2
00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:08.707
In this unit you will learn all about time and daily life.

3
00:00:08.787 --> 00:00:13.794
You will name the twelve months
of the year and put them in order.

4
00:00:13.874 --> 00:00:19.728
You will talk about your daily routine
— what you do at each hour of the day.

5
00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:27.436
You will learn to talk about the past (what already
happened), the present (what happens every day),

6
00:00:27.516 --> 00:00:30.442
and the future (what will happen soon).

7
00:00:30.522 --> 00:00:37.918
By the end, you will be able to make your own
calendar, just like Amal does in this unit’s story,

8
00:00:37.998 --> 00:00:39.999
“The Calendar on the Wall”.

9
00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:46.473
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:46.553 --> 00:00:54.828
By the end of this unit, you will be able to name
and spell the twelve months of the year, in order.

11
00:00:54.908 --> 00:01:00.759
You will also talk about your daily
routine using the hours of the day.

12
00:01:00.839 --> 00:01:06.858
You will also use the Present Simple
to talk about what you do every day.

13
00:01:06.938 --> 00:01:09.949
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:10.029 --> 00:01:15.573
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
January, February, March, April, May, June.

15
00:01:15.653 --> 00:01:20.690
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:24.671
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:29.140
The reading sequence includes My Day,
Hour by Hour and The Twelve Months.

18
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:29.997
Read actively.

19
00:01:30.077 --> 00:01:34.221
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:34.301 --> 00:01:38.199
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:38.279 --> 00:01:41.139
The first language focus is Present Simple.

22
00:01:41.219 --> 00:01:48.866
We use the Present Simple to talk about things that happen
again and again, and about facts that are always true.

23
00:01:48.946 --> 00:01:50.643
It is the “routine tense”.

24
00:01:50.723 --> 00:01:56.250
When we say “She marks the calendar every
morning,” we mean she does it regularly.

25
00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:59.395
This helps us describe our daily life clearly.

26
00:01:59.475 --> 00:02:06.848
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:06.928 --> 00:02:10.823
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1 — My Daily Routine.

28
00:02:10.903 --> 00:02:15.190
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.514
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:18.594 --> 00:02:21.968
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:22.048 --> 00:02:23.531
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:23.611 --> 00:02:30.260
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.522
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:33.602 --> 00:02:41.066
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
